```python
import math

def vswr(zl, z0=50.0):
    g = abs((zl - z0) / (zl + z0))
    if g >= 1 - 1e-12:
        return math.inf
    return (1 + g) / (1 - g)
```

The magnitude of $\Gamma$ is all that matters, which is why 100 Ω and 25 Ω are equally bad on a 50 Ω line ($|\Gamma| = 1/3$, VSWR 2), and why $100 + j50$ and $50 - j50$ (which, as the Smith-chart example showed, are $\lambda/8$ apart on the same line) have the same VSWR, 2.618. A pure reactance $jX$ has $|jX - Z_0| = |jX + Z_0|$, so $|\Gamma| = 1$ exactly in theory; in floating point it can come out as 0.9999999999999999, hence the tolerance.
